<h3>4.2 Inputs and criteria</h3>
<p>Water depth is {{water_depth_m}} m; reservoir size is {{reservoir_size_mmbbl}} million
barrels; production capacity is {{production_capacity_bopd}} barrels per day; price is
{{oil_price_usd_per_bbl}} USD per barrel; annual discount rate is {{discount_rate}} as a
fraction; and field life is {{field_life_years}} years. These are illustrative assumptions,
not verified field measurements. Table A supplies the input register.</p>
<p>The depth band is the host exclusion criterion implemented by the ranking model.
The zero-value line follows the definition of net present value. Neither criterion is
a code-of-practice acceptance limit. No standard-derived structural or installation
criterion is claimed.</p>

<h2 id="method">5. Theory and methodology</h2>
<p>Host suitability is calculated using weighted depth, reservoir, distance and fluid
scores. Cost estimates are then converted into an annual cash-flow sequence and discounted.
The mathematical definitions below describe the implemented local calculation, whose
source editions are recorded in §9.2.</p>
<h3>5.1 Concept ranking and cost estimation</h3>
<div class="eq">S = w<sub>d</sub>s<sub>d</sub> + w<sub>r</sub>s<sub>r</sub>
+ w<sub>l</sub>s<sub>l</sub> + w<sub>f</sub>s<sub>f</sub></div>
<p>Here S is dimensionless suitability; s denotes a component score; w denotes its
implemented weight; and subscripts d, r, l and f denote depth, reservoir size,
infrastructure distance and fluid type. A host outside its hard depth band receives a
zero score before the weighted sum. These are engineering heuristics, not calibrated
probabilities. Depth, reservoir, distance and fluid weights are respectively
{{weight_depth}}, {{weight_reservoir}}, {{weight_distance}} and {{weight_fluid}}
(<code>concept_selection.py</code>, composite score). These values are
ASSUMED - to be confirmed, based on previous experience; their independent empirical
calibration is NOT_EVALUATED.</p>
<div class="eq">C = C<sub>ref</sub>(Q/Q<sub>ref</sub>)<sup>a</sup>
[1 + k max(0, d − d<sub>ref</sub>)]<br>
O = O<sub>ref</sub>(Q/Q<sub>ref</sub>)<sup>b</sup>A(age)</div>
<p>C is standalone-host capital expenditure; O is annual operating expenditure;
Q is daily production capacity; d is water depth; ref denotes the implemented reference
condition; a and b are capacity exponents; k is the depth coefficient; and A is the
age multiplier. The economic fallback uses the base cost estimates and the reference
operating age. Abandonment expenditure is a fixed fraction of capital expenditure when
no external estimate or override is available. That fraction is {{abandonment_fraction}}
(<code>economics.py</code>, <code>evaluate_economics</code>),
ASSUMED - to be confirmed, based on previous experience. The historical cost basis is recorded in
the source; escalation, location adjustment and quotation accuracy are not established.</p>
<h3>5.2 Production and discounted cash flow</h3>
<div class="eq">q<sub>t</sub> = Qf<sub>t</sub>; &nbsp;
f<sub>t</sub> = exp(−Dτ) after the plateau<br>
F<sub>t</sub> = R<sub>t</sub> − O<sub>t</sub> − C<sub>t</sub> − A<sub>t</sub>;<br>
V = Σ<sub>t</sub> F<sub>t</sub>/(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></div>
<p>q is daily production rate; f is the production multiplier; D is exponential decline
rate; τ is elapsed time after the plateau in years; F is annual net cash flow; R is
revenue; C is capital expenditure; O is operating expenditure; A is abandonment cost;
r is annual discount rate; and V is net present value. The time index t includes the
initial development period. Monetary cash flows are in million USD.</p>
<p>Supplying reservoir size activates automatic exponential decline in this workflow.
The recovery factor is {{recovery_factor}} and the plateau fraction of field life is
{{plateau_fraction}}, ASSUMED - to be confirmed, based on previous experience
(<code>economics.py</code>, <code>EconomicsInput</code>,
<code>_derive_decline_rate_from_eur</code> and <code>_production_factors</code>).
Estimated ultimate recovery (EUR) is {{eur_mmbbl}} million barrels: reservoir size
multiplied by the recovery factor. The {{plateau_years}}-year plateau alone produces
{{plateau_mmbbl}} million barrels, exceeding that EUR before decline begins.
The decline rate {{decline_rate}} per year is therefore a fixed fallback constant,
not a rate derived to satisfy EUR. The implemented plateau remains
at capacity even when its cumulative volume exceeds the recovery target. Operating
expenditure falls with the production multiplier; fixed operating costs are not separated.
Capital spending overlaps early production, and abandonment is charged in the final year.
Carbon cost is omitted because no carbon-price input is supplied.</p>

<h2 id="results">6. Worked example results</h2>
<p>The documented example is evaluated through the public workflow. Spar and
Semi-submersible tie at {{score_Semi}} each. Stable legacy enumeration order places
{{selected}} first; the ordered scores are retained in Table B. The tie-break is an
implementation convention, not a demonstrated engineering preference.</p>
<p>Calculated capital expenditure is {{capital}} million USD. Reference annual operating
expenditure is {{operating}} million USD/year, and abandonment expenditure is
{{abandonment}} million USD. Pre-tax net present value is {{npv}} million USD, above
the zero-value comparison line. These estimates inherit the unverified cost calibration
and the production discrepancy. For the tied Semi-submersible, the public
<code>compare_concepts</code> call gives capital expenditure {{tied_capital}} million USD,
annual operating expenditure {{tied_operating}} million USD/year, abandonment expenditure
{{tied_abandonment}} million USD, and net present value {{tied_npv}} million USD.
The concepts share the production limitation; the two NPVs are separate cost cases,
not a basis for investment selection.</p>
<p>Scheduled production is {{production_mmbbl}} million barrels, exceeding the supplied
reservoir size of {{reservoir_size_mmbbl}} million barrels by {{excess_percent}}% relative
to that supplied size. Production is {{production_to_eur}} times the module EUR of
{{eur_mmbbl}} million barrels. Reservoir size is described as recoverable reserves by the ranking
interface, while the economics calculation applies a further recovery-factor assumption.
The inconsistent meaning and the uncapped plateau require reconciliation before this
economic value is used for a development decision.</p>

<h2 id="validation">7. Validation evidence</h2>
<p>The repository evidence is separated into software regression, illustrative examples
and independent engineering validation. Workflow tests check input rejection, populated
outputs, finite economic values, selected-host consistency and comparison ordering.</p>
<p>Cost tests check scaling, ordering and invalid-input handling. Concept tests exercise
ranking and exclusion behaviour. Economics tests include mocked adapters and optional
integration paths; their existence does not establish the accuracy of the external cost
or fiscal models. Benchmark tests include explicitly synthetic records. The public
reference catalogue supplies descriptive analogues, but no independent measured
cash-flow comparison for this worked example is established.</p>
<p>Report verification compares displayed values and plotted samples with fresh module
returns and hand-worked literal expectations for capital, operating and abandonment costs,
production, suitability scores and all sensitivity values. An independent Decimal schedule
checks the arithmetic without private model helpers; a separate standard-library schedule
is reconciled to module NPV. The reference production is reconstructed from the cited
schedule because the public result does not expose cumulative production.
This is a reproducibility check, not independent validation of reserves, costs or tax.</p>

<h2 id="limits">8. Key findings, limits and recommendations</h2>
<p>The calculation supports comparative screening under explicit assumptions.
Investment acceptance remains NOT_EVALUATED because production conservation, cost
calibration and fiscal suitability are not established.</p>
<p>Reconcile the reserve definition and constrain cumulative production to an independently
approved recoverable-volume forecast before interpreting economic value. Add a regression
criterion that cumulative production does not exceed that forecast. This action follows
the discrepancy in §6 and the production construction identified in §9.2.</p>
<p>Provide priced capital, operating and abandonment inputs with a stated currency date
before financial use. Verify the selected fiscal calculation against the actual offshore
regime; the local fallback does not apply tax, and the optional United States adapter
selects an onshore implementation. Record the actual backend with each evaluation.
These actions follow the adapter selection and cost fallback evidence in §9.2.</p>
<p>Reject an all-zero concept ranking before further evaluation. The single-concept
workflow can still select the first listed host when every host is excluded; the
comparison workflow explicitly rejects that condition. For tiebacks, pass the actual
distance into a separately verified cost basis: the local economic fallback substitutes
a fixed distance rather than forwarding the screening distance.</p>
<h3>8.1 Not covered</h3>
<p>Generalized onshore and offshore layout routing, cable and flowline sizing, rig
capability, empirical concept probabilities, architecture statistics, schedule analytics,
multi-axis concept screening and visualization are available as separate sub-areas.
They are not evaluated here. Installation orientation, sea-state limits, rigging,
forecast support, structural capacity and operational envelopes require other analyses
and supplied design data. No installation limit is inferred from this economic example.</p>

<h2 id="references">9. Basis records and references</h2>
<p>The evidence is identified by repository text edition. The cited records describe
implemented behaviour; they are not substitutes for licensed design standards or
independent cost calibration.</p>
<h3>9.1 Evidence interpretation</h3>
<p>ASSUMED - to be confirmed applies to the illustrative inputs and uncalibrated model
heuristics. NOT_EVALUATED means the necessary acceptance evidence is absent. An actual
zero denotes a numerical value; it is not used for missing data. Source text digests use
UTF-8 with normalized line endings so the cited edition is reproducible.</p>
<h3>9.2 Source locators and editions</h3>
<p>The numerical input source is <code>workflow.py</code>, module Usage example.
The public call is <code>evaluate_field_development(FieldDevelopmentSpec(...))</code>.
The rank equation is implemented in <code>concept_selection._composite_score</code>;
limits are in <code>_DEPTH_LIMITS</code>. Cost correlations are in
<code>capex_estimator._capacity_scale</code>, <code>_depth_scale</code>,
<code>_BASE_CAPEX</code> and <code>opex_estimator._capacity_opex_scale</code>,
<code>_age_factor</code>, <code>_BASE_OPEX_MM_YR</code>.</p>
<p>The production definitions are in <code>economics.EconomicsInput.__post_init__</code>,
<code>_derive_decline_rate_from_eur</code> and <code>_production_factors</code>.
Cash flow and discounting are in <code>_build_annual_cashflows</code> and
<code>_numpy_financial_metrics</code>. The cash-flow allocation is 0.30, 0.50 and 0.20
of capital expenditure in years zero, one and two; annual conversion uses 365 days
(<code>economics.py</code>, <code>_build_annual_cashflows</code>).
Reference arithmetic in <code>build_report.py</code> follows those model definitions;
the model source, rather than this report, supplies each coefficient. Adapter selection is in
<code>_resolve_capex_adapter</code>, <code>_resolve_fiscal_calculator</code> and
<code>evaluate_economics</code>. The source edition for every quoted coefficient or
limit is the corresponding text digest below.</p>
<ol class="refs">{{references}}</ol>

